WEBVTT

00:01.708 --> 00:10.091
[SPEAKER_00]: Leslie takes out the annotated version of the magnum opus that his master provided, okay?

00:11.591 --> 00:20.334
[SPEAKER_00]: This was Al-Bashon's personal working copy of the magnum opus of Ujor Udias, the foundational treaty on transplantation, written in ancient Seul.

00:21.474 --> 00:25.755
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the book that Al-Bashon taught you from, okay?

00:25.875 --> 00:30.017
[SPEAKER_00]: It is obviously not the original magnum opus, but one that is

00:32.648 --> 00:33.068
[SPEAKER_00]: Edited.

00:33.128 --> 00:47.757
[SPEAKER_00]: It was the edited one and the version that The Office of Forbidden Magic produced after they edited it.

00:47.837 --> 00:52.680
[SPEAKER_00]: The book is probably as old as Albishan himself when he became a transmitter.

00:53.441 --> 00:56.983
[SPEAKER_00]: He owned it and worked in it most of his life and turned it into a record of his own work.

00:58.212 --> 01:16.829
[SPEAKER_00]: So this doesn't contain any spells, but its value is entirely on his thoughts of transmutation because what he did was he would take copies of the magnum opus and interleave it with his own studies and research into transmutation.

01:17.970 --> 01:24.095
[SPEAKER_00]: So really it's kind of his interpretation of the magnum opus and expounding on things

01:24.856 --> 01:27.479
[SPEAKER_00]: That he learned over the years.

01:28.340 --> 01:34.847
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, the binding is a large heavy folio that he had spiced together and rebound himself many times.

01:35.468 --> 01:46.580
[SPEAKER_00]: The current covers are scuffed calfskin over boards, the spine has been repaired with linen and tape, and the leather is worn pale where the thumb has opened it thousands of times.

01:48.625 --> 01:49.946
[SPEAKER_00]: There's two layers of text.

01:50.427 --> 02:01.237
[SPEAKER_00]: Ushur's text in a formal ancient school book hand is a deliberately obscure, built on allegory, metaphoric, coded terms such as the green lion, the king's bath, and the bread and wine.

02:01.737 --> 02:05.601
[SPEAKER_00]: The obscurity is why the magnum opus has a reputation of being nearly unreadable.

02:06.562 --> 02:09.205
[SPEAKER_00]: Al-Bashan notes are mostly in common

02:10.598 --> 02:15.284
[SPEAKER_00]: The plain trade tongue with Sewell technical terms kept and glossed.

02:15.745 --> 02:25.378
[SPEAKER_00]: He wrote them so that a student, almost certainly you, could follow them easily, especially with your knowledge of ancient Sewell.

02:26.850 --> 02:38.793
[SPEAKER_00]: They put Ujur's allegories into practical terms where he says things like, the green dragon devours its tail means at the bench, at what temperature, in which vessel, and for how long.

02:39.173 --> 02:44.174
[SPEAKER_00]: So he's basically telling you how to interpret this book.

02:44.214 --> 02:47.134
[SPEAKER_02]: This is like, you're an Iridian, right?

02:47.715 --> 02:53.956
[SPEAKER_00]: He's translated this into your language, even though you know ancient Seul very well.

02:57.191 --> 02:59.833
[SPEAKER_00]: The notes span decades and the ink changes with them.

03:00.053 --> 03:05.617
[SPEAKER_00]: Iron gall, gone brown in the oldest notes, then black, then a sharp blue-black is in the most recent.

03:06.017 --> 03:09.560
[SPEAKER_00]: A reader can see that Albichon's thinking changed over time.

03:10.120 --> 03:12.922
[SPEAKER_00]: Some early notes are crossed out with the later notes beside them.

03:14.615 --> 03:15.596
[SPEAKER_00]: Experimental records.

03:15.656 --> 03:23.064
[SPEAKER_00]: Throughout the book, Alvashad recorded his own trials with the date, the materials, the quantities, the results, and a mark of success or failure.

03:23.784 --> 03:26.427
[SPEAKER_00]: Failures outnumber successes, and he recorded both.

03:26.848 --> 03:33.875
[SPEAKER_00]: Some entries have small diagrams of apparatus, retorts, an alfanor, which is a furnace, and a pelican vessel.

03:35.523 --> 03:38.104
[SPEAKER_00]: Abashan clearly knew the text had been censored.

03:38.944 --> 03:48.966
[SPEAKER_00]: The office cuts are crude, passages that stop an argument, cross-references the sections that no longer exist, and a change of copying hand where material was patched over.

03:49.566 --> 03:52.947
[SPEAKER_00]: Abashan marks each of these places in the margin, for example.

03:53.407 --> 03:55.447
[SPEAKER_00]: Cut here, or something was removed.

03:55.887 --> 03:57.348
[SPEAKER_00]: The argument does not join.

03:57.748 --> 04:03.549
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, he's saying the original magnum opus has been edited, censored,

04:04.369 --> 04:06.970
[SPEAKER_00]: And modified from its original version.

04:08.650 --> 04:12.512
[SPEAKER_00]: He notes gaps, but does not claim to know what was taken out.

04:12.592 --> 04:18.273
[SPEAKER_00]: In particular, he does not talk about Immoculus.

04:20.834 --> 04:26.396
[SPEAKER_00]: Chemical stains, wax drips, and scorches are along one end, and the pages are soft with hardening.

04:26.896 --> 04:29.817
[SPEAKER_00]: It survived the fire because it was inside the stone box.

04:30.497 --> 04:32.538
[SPEAKER_00]: It smells of sulfur and old smoke.

04:41.397 --> 04:46.083
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, you're looking for... Explosive powder.

04:46.483 --> 04:47.405
[SPEAKER_00]: Explosive powder.

04:52.611 --> 05:00.120
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything thus far on anything to do with like copper or sahuagin or any of the...

05:01.620 --> 05:05.182
[SPEAKER_01]: Sulfur, powder, anything on protective powder so far with you?

05:05.542 --> 05:06.003
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't think so.

05:06.063 --> 05:07.143
[SPEAKER_01]: I've heard a reference to it.

05:07.204 --> 05:12.707
[SPEAKER_01]: He was trying to get his hands on it to study himself before I brought him the sample.

05:14.108 --> 05:14.268
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah.

05:15.288 --> 05:16.749
[SPEAKER_01]: Roll this.

05:19.071 --> 05:19.331
[SPEAKER_00]: Ten.

05:19.811 --> 05:20.091
[SPEAKER_00]: Ten.

05:20.412 --> 05:23.614
[SPEAKER_00]: Plus, what is your modification for Arcana?

05:24.034 --> 05:24.494
[SPEAKER_00]: For what?

05:24.674 --> 05:25.215
[SPEAKER_00]: Arcana.

05:26.696 --> 05:28.857
[SPEAKER_01]: So it would be a skill that's right here, Ro.

05:30.445 --> 05:34.629
[SPEAKER_01]: And it'll probably be, so arcane is listed here on the skills section.

05:35.129 --> 05:35.709
[SPEAKER_02]: Plus six.

05:36.250 --> 05:36.730
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

05:38.612 --> 05:42.195
[SPEAKER_02]: So 16.

05:42.215 --> 05:51.362
[SPEAKER_00]: So you know that if your master was working on that stuff, it would probably be recently, right?

05:52.263 --> 05:58.809
[SPEAKER_00]: You might have mentioned some of it somewhere, but you turn to the back pages.

06:09.577 --> 06:11.738
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing on blasting powder, unfortunately.

06:12.999 --> 06:22.644
[SPEAKER_00]: However, and nothing on this strange copper and sulfur mixture, even though he does talk about sulfuric compounds.

06:23.385 --> 06:33.510
[SPEAKER_00]: However, in the last leaves he talks about something called the Gift of Insamyar, which you guys have heard of, something he had been mumbling about for months.

06:36.795 --> 06:42.820
[SPEAKER_00]: After their chapter on Rubido, which is a final color of alchemy,

06:45.023 --> 06:54.708
[SPEAKER_00]: On the final blank leaves, at the back of the book, Al-Bashan kept a few short notes on the gift of Insimiar, which he was studying but had made little progress on.

06:55.368 --> 07:05.794
[SPEAKER_00]: They are dated in the blue-black ink of his last years, and very brief, a handful of lines and observations, with nothing worked out and no experiment carried out.

07:06.414 --> 07:11.597
[SPEAKER_00]: They are not keyed to any passages of Eugor's texts, and add nothing to the book's teaching.

07:12.477 --> 07:21.062
[SPEAKER_00]: The last of them is underlying twice.

07:38.051 --> 07:41.593
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, he says that the gift of Nsehemiar

07:43.037 --> 07:46.101
[SPEAKER_00]: He needs a sample of it to be able to look into what it is.

07:47.262 --> 07:57.054
[SPEAKER_00]: But he was very worried about it, and he thinks this stuff is what is causing the people of the Warren to become more rational and violent as well.

07:58.076 --> 07:59.457
[SPEAKER_00]: That's an interesting question.

08:00.018 --> 08:01.520
[SPEAKER_02]: Are they also violent at the same time?

08:07.266 --> 08:07.786
[SPEAKER_02]: Nesimiar?

08:08.387 --> 08:11.570
[SPEAKER_02]: Nesimiar is a legendary black dragon, right?

08:12.010 --> 08:20.458
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no, what the gift of Nesimiar is supposed to be is like this dragon is the thing, okay?

08:20.898 --> 08:25.743
[SPEAKER_00]: Kind of, it links back to the people that are in the dungeon, right?

08:26.583 --> 08:30.167
[SPEAKER_00]: And he talks about the fact that he thinks that there are a group of people

08:31.612 --> 08:39.575
[SPEAKER_00]: That are bringing the gift of Insamia into the city.

08:40.015 --> 08:42.916
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't know who it is, but it seems like it's coming in.

08:43.876 --> 08:52.039
[SPEAKER_00]: What the book does not contain, there's no powder recipe, and there's no cure for the gift of Insamia.

08:52.719 --> 08:59.142
[SPEAKER_00]: However, he speaks about the fact that he's created a treatment for it in his potion scrolls.

09:00.057 --> 09:02.238
[SPEAKER_00]: So you know that the scrolls are about potions.

09:05.280 --> 09:13.445
[SPEAKER_02]: My control is another way to get people looking.

09:14.365 --> 09:19.508
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what he said about the gift.

09:22.069 --> 09:25.511
[SPEAKER_00]: I do not yet understand what he means by the willing gift.

09:27.292 --> 09:28.193
[SPEAKER_00]: Underlined twice.

09:52.498 --> 09:55.619
[SPEAKER_00]: All right, what do you teach to do next?

09:56.319 --> 09:59.620
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, let me tell you about what this book does for you, okay?

10:01.320 --> 10:13.043
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, when you become skilled enough, like when you're training from fourth to fifth level, if you have this book with you, it halves the amount of time that it takes for you to go up to the next level.

10:13.943 --> 10:16.464
[SPEAKER_00]: So training time is reduced by half.

10:19.105 --> 10:19.345
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

10:29.403 --> 10:38.986
[SPEAKER_00]: However, for this to be the case, you must be a transmuter, you must read into Sewell, and you must have access to the book during training.

10:51.350 --> 10:56.431
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so, what do you wish to look at next?

11:02.404 --> 11:07.585
[SPEAKER_02]: So there were three books, there was the potions, the journal, and what else?

11:07.965 --> 11:13.707
[SPEAKER_02]: Three books, eight potions, and eight scrolls.

11:14.567 --> 11:19.848
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there like a paper that has the potions and stuff I can see?

11:20.309 --> 11:22.109
[SPEAKER_00]: The scrolls, if you want to look at the scrolls.

11:22.749 --> 11:22.949
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

11:23.389 --> 11:25.470
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there any particular scroll that you want to look at?

11:26.993 --> 11:29.234
[SPEAKER_02]: Uh, probably just read through them.

11:29.555 --> 11:29.875
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

11:30.135 --> 11:36.719
[SPEAKER_01]: If you could tell me what those scrolls are so that I could log them, don't cast them no matter what, right?

11:36.839 --> 11:40.200
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the scroll will disappear if they happen to be a spell.

11:40.220 --> 11:43.062
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can still investigate until I look to see what it is.

11:44.183 --> 11:50.946
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't understand it though, like if you can't read it for some reason, it might just be more of a skill level thing, just set it to the side and I'll take a look at it myself.

11:51.267 --> 11:52.727
[SPEAKER_02]: Leslie understands everything.

11:55.189 --> 11:55.529
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

11:56.812 --> 12:00.154
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so you pick a scroll, right?

12:03.696 --> 12:08.619
[SPEAKER_00]: And you study it.

12:08.639 --> 12:09.759
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you pick a scroll at random?

12:10.260 --> 12:10.500
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

12:10.780 --> 12:12.201
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, roll an eight-sided die.

12:17.664 --> 12:18.384
[SPEAKER_02]: It looks like this.

12:22.266 --> 12:24.948
[SPEAKER_02]: I thought there was a nine on here, but it was a six.

12:26.307 --> 12:26.648
[SPEAKER_02]: Seven.

12:27.209 --> 12:31.375
[SPEAKER_02]: Let me see the pen.

12:37.626 --> 12:40.550
[SPEAKER_00]: Alright, so you pick up one and you start reading it.

12:42.324 --> 12:45.526
[SPEAKER_00]: Roll your intelligence check at advantage.

12:45.686 --> 12:51.410
[SPEAKER_00]: That means use two 20-sideds and you want your arcanum scroll to be high.

12:52.350 --> 12:55.132
[SPEAKER_00]: This is a DC-15 roll.

12:55.552 --> 12:57.053
[SPEAKER_00]: So roll a 20-sided two times?

12:57.194 --> 12:59.275
[SPEAKER_00]: Two times and add your arcanum onto it.

13:00.552 --> 13:01.012
[SPEAKER_02]: Arcana.

13:01.833 --> 13:04.115
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that your plus six?

13:04.615 --> 13:05.156
[SPEAKER_02]: Nat 20.

13:05.536 --> 13:06.096
[SPEAKER_02]: Nat 20?

13:06.216 --> 13:07.117
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

13:07.257 --> 13:09.579
[SPEAKER_00]: You look at the scroll and you know instantly what it is.

13:10.379 --> 13:16.104
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a recipe for a potion known as the oil of slipperiness.

13:17.004 --> 13:17.325
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay?

13:18.105 --> 13:21.809
[SPEAKER_00]: When you put it on the skin, it gives freedom of movement and lasts for eight hours.

13:21.889 --> 13:23.831
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically nobody can grab you or touch you.

13:24.491 --> 13:25.412
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't be paralyzed.

13:25.452 --> 13:34.241
[SPEAKER_00]: Pour it on the ground, it makes a slippery patch of greasy magic for eight hours, which basically means anyone that steps on it will slip.

13:34.541 --> 13:38.485
[SPEAKER_02]: Question about the buttery thing.

13:38.945 --> 13:41.748
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you get shot by arrows or does it deflect?

13:43.672 --> 13:45.094
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, slide off the company.

13:45.334 --> 13:45.875
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

13:45.895 --> 13:48.858
[SPEAKER_00]: But basically, it means that nobody can grab you.

13:49.238 --> 13:50.860
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how I can sleep on the streets.

13:51.181 --> 13:58.669
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, your master would actually make this to be able to make mechanical things and he would use the oil

13:59.530 --> 14:03.712
